Looking at this problem, I need to determine whether a noncyclic group can have a totally ordered (chain) subgroup lattice.

## Proof

**Yes**, such a group exists. Consider the **Prüfer $p$-group** (also called the quasicyclic group) $\mathbb{Z}(p^\infty)$ for any prime $p$.

**Definition.** Let
$$\mathbb{Z}(p^\infty) = \{z \in \mathbb{C}^\times : z^{p^n} = 1 \text{ for some } n \geq 0\},$$
the group of all $p$-power roots of unity under multiplication.

**The subgroup lattice is totally ordered.** I claim the subgroups of $\mathbb{Z}(p^\infty)$ are exactly:
$$\{1\} \subset \mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z} \subset \mathbb{Z}/p^2\mathbb{Z} \subset \mathbb{Z}/p^3\mathbb{Z} \subset \cdots \subset \mathbb{Z}(p^\infty),$$
where $\mathbb{Z}/p^n\mathbb{Z} := \{z : z^{p^n} = 1\}$ is the unique subgroup of order $p^n$.

*Proof of the claim.* Let $H \leq \mathbb{Z}(p^\infty)$ be a proper subgroup. Define
$$m = \sup\{n \geq 0 : H \text{ contains an element of order } p^n\}.$$

- **If $m < \infty$:** Every element of $H$ has order dividing $p^m$, so $H \subseteq \{z : z^{p^m}=1\} = \mathbb{Z}/p^m\mathbb{Z}$. Conversely, $H$ contains an element of order $p^m$, and since $\mathbb{Z}/p^m\mathbb{Z}$ is the unique cyclic subgroup of order $p^m$ (all elements of order dividing $p^m$ form this single subgroup), $H$ contains $\mathbb{Z}/p^m\mathbb{Z}$. Thus $H = \mathbb{Z}/p^m\mathbb{Z}$.

- **If $m = \infty$:** $H$ contains elements of order $p^n$ for every $n$, hence contains $\mathbb{Z}/p^n\mathbb{Z}$ for every $n$. Their union is all of $\mathbb{Z}(p^\infty)$, so $H = \mathbb{Z}(p^\infty)$, contradicting properness.

Thus every proper subgroup is $\mathbb{Z}/p^n\mathbb{Z}$ for some $n$, and these are linearly ordered by inclusion. The full subgroup lattice is a chain. $\square$

**The group is noncyclic.** The group $\mathbb{Z}(p^\infty)$ is infinite (it contains elements of order $p^n$ for every $n$), yet every element has finite order. A cyclic group is either finite or isomorphic to $\mathbb{Z}$; the latter has elements of infinite order. Since $\mathbb{Z}(p^\infty)$ is infinite but has no element of infinite order, it is **not cyclic**.

**Conclusion.** The Prüfer $p$-group $\mathbb{Z}(p^\infty)$ is a noncyclic group whose subgroup lattice is totally ordered. Therefore, such a group exists.

